---
/**
 * Terms of sale.
 *
 * Written in plain English rather than in legalese, because the person
 * reading it is buying a tray of pastry from a student with a home kitchen,
 * and a page of defined terms would be theatre.
 *
 * TODO, for Hakop: nothing on this page has been read by a lawyer. It states
 * only things that are already true of how the site works, and it makes no
 * promise the kitchen cannot keep, but a review before the store opens would
 * be cheap insurance. The refunds page is the one with an open decision on it.
 */
import LegalLayout from "@components/LegalLayout.astro";
import { business, commerce, compliance, complianceIsComplete, storeOpen } from "@config/site";

const rawHour = commerce.cutoff.hour;
const cutoffHour = rawHour % 12 === 0 ? 12 : rawHour % 12;
const cutoffLabel = `${cutoffHour}${rawHour < 12 ? "am" : "pm"}`;
/* A number, not the config literal, so the plural survives a change. */
const daysBefore: number = commerce.cutoff.daysBefore;
const dayWord = daysBefore === 1 ? "day" : "days";

const TOC = [
  { id: "who", label: "Who you are buying from" },
  { id: "california", label: "California only" },
  { id: "ordering", label: "How an order works" },
  { id: "made-to-order", label: "Bake dates and the cutoff" },
  { id: "allergens", label: "Allergens" },
  { id: "getting-it", label: "Pickup, delivery and shipping" },
  { id: "after", label: "Once the tray is yours" },
  { id: "resale", label: "Trays are for eating, not reselling" },
  { id: "prices", label: "Prices and availability" },
  { id: "wrong", label: "When something goes wrong" },
  { id: "site", label: "The site itself" },
  { id: "law", label: "Which law applies" },
];
---

<LegalLayout
  title="Terms of sale"
  description="The terms you buy under: a Class A cottage food operation in Cypress, California, selling direct to customers inside California only, with orders made to order against a bake date."
  updated="2026-09-11"
  lede="What you are agreeing to when you order. It is short, and it says only things that are already true of how this bakery works."
  toc={TOC}
>
  <h2 id="who">Who you are buying from</h2>

  <p>
    {business.name} is {business.owner}, working from a home kitchen
    in {business.city}, California. It is a Class {compliance.operationClass}{" "}
    Cottage Food Operation, which is a category in California law for food made
    at home and sold directly to the person eating it.
  </p>

  {
    complianceIsComplete ? (
      <p>
        The kitchen is registered with {compliance.county}. The registration
        number appears at the foot of every page on this site and on every
        label.
      </p>
    ) : (
      <p class="unsettled">
        <span class="unsettled-title">Registration is pending.</span> The
        application is with {compliance.county} and no number has been issued
        yet. Until it is, nothing can be ordered and nothing can be sold. The
        foot of every page says the same thing.
      </p>
    )
  }

  <h2 id="california">California only</h2>

  <p>
    Everything sold here stays inside California. Cottage food law does not
    allow this kitchen to sell across a state line, so there is no shipping to
    the rest of the country, and there is no exception for a gift or a friend.
  </p>

  <p>
    The address is checked before checkout, checked again by the payment page,
    and checked a third time after payment against the address you actually
    entered. If an order comes through with an address outside California it is
    refunded in full automatically and you are emailed to say why. Nothing is
    kept.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ordering">How an order works</h2>

  <p>
    You choose a tray, a bake date and how you want to receive it, and you pay
    through Stripe. The price you pay is the price the server works out from
    the catalog, not a number sent from your browser, so the amount on the
    payment page is the amount.
  </p>

  <p>
    An order is confirmed when the payment completes and you get a confirmation
    with a reference on it. Until then nothing is reserved. If a bake date
    fills up while you are checking out, the order is refused before payment
    rather than after it.
  </p>

  <p>
    {business.owner} can refuse an order. That is rare and it is usually
    capacity: one person with one oven cannot bake everything that is asked
    for. A refused order is refunded in full.
  </p>

  <h2 id="made-to-order">Bake dates and the cutoff</h2>

  <p>
    Nothing here is sitting on a shelf. You order against a bake date and the
    tray is made for that date.
  </p>

  <p>
    Orders for a date close at <span class="tabular">{cutoffLabel}</span>,{" "}
    <span class="tabular">{daysBefore}</span> {dayWord} before it. Tuesday is
    never a bake day, because {business.owner} is in class. What you can cancel
    and when is on the{" "}
    <a href="/legal/refunds">refunds and cancellations page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="allergens">Allergens</h2>

  <div class="pullout">
    <p>
      <strong>Every tray contains wheat, milk, egg, almonds, walnuts and
      sesame</strong>, and it is baked in a home kitchen that handles all six,
      so cross contact is possible. Read the{" "}
      <a href="/legal/allergens">full allergen statement</a> before you order
      for anybody with an allergy.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    By ordering you are confirming that you have read what is in it. The
    ingredient list on the product page, on this site and on the printed label
    is the same list.
  </p>

  <h2 id="getting-it">Pickup, delivery and shipping</h2>

  <p>
    For pickup, come at the time agreed for that bake date. Orders nobody
    collects are covered on the refunds page.
  </p>

  <p>
    For local delivery, the tray goes to the address on the order and somebody
    needs to be able to take it. For shipping, it goes to a California address
    by carrier, and how long a parcel spends in transit is up to the carrier.
  </p>

  <h2 id="after">Once the tray is yours</h2>

  <p>
    Keep it the way the label says: sealed, at room temperature, out of direct
    sun. Once it has left {business.owner}'s hands, how it is stored is yours.
  </p>

  <p>
    If something is wrong with the food itself, that is not your fault and it
    is not treated as though it is. See the{" "}
    <a href="/legal/refunds">refunds page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="resale">Trays are for eating, not reselling</h2>

  <p>
    A Class {compliance.operationClass} cottage food operation may only sell
    directly to the person who is going to eat the food. That is the law rather
    than a preference, so a tray bought here cannot be resold in a shop, a
    cafe, a market stall or a catering job.
  </p>

  <p>
    Buying a tray for a party, an office or a family gathering is exactly what
    it is for. Reselling it is the thing that is not allowed.
  </p>

  <h2 id="prices">Prices and availability</h2>

  <p>
    Prices can change, and a bake date can fill up or be cancelled. The site is
    the current word on both. An order already paid for keeps the price it was
    paid at.
  </p>

  {
    !storeOpen && (
      <p class="unsettled">
        <span class="unsettled-title">Nothing is for sale today.</span> The
        store is closed, so no figure shown anywhere on this site is a price
        you can pay.
      </p>
    )
  }

  <p>
    California sales tax is worked out at checkout from the address you give,
    where it applies.
  </p>

  <h2 id="wrong">When something goes wrong</h2>

  <p>
    If a tray is wrong, late, damaged or never arrives, you get a replacement
    or your money back. That is the remedy, and it is the most this bakery can
    do: it is one person selling pastry, and it cannot cover a loss larger than
    what you paid for the order.
  </p>

  <p>
    Nothing here limits anything California law does not let it limit, and
    nothing here takes away a right you have as a consumer.
  </p>

  <h2 id="site">The site itself</h2>

  <p>
    The photographs on this site are of the real product and they belong to
    {" "}{business.owner}, along with the words around them. Please do not
    reuse them to advertise something else.
  </p>

  <h2 id="law">Which law applies</h2>

  <p>
    California law, and the courts of Orange County, California. Since
    everything sold here is sold inside California, that is the only sensible
    answer.
  </p>

  <p>
    If part of this page turned out to be unenforceable, the rest of it would
    still stand.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
